research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-54-$$\sum_{j \ge 0} \frac{(-1)^{j}}{j+1} \;=\; \sum_j \varepsilon_j b_j .$$
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-55-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-56-It **converges conditionally** ([[def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence]]):
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-57-it converges, by the alternating series test, while its series of absolute values
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-58-is the harmonic series $\sum_{k\ge1} 1/k$, which diverges
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-59-([[thm-p-series-rational]]). Writing $S$ for its sum,
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-60-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-61-$$\tfrac{1}{2} \;<\; \tfrac{7}{12} \;\le\; S \;\le\; \tfrac{5}{6} \;<\; 1 .$$
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-62-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-63-**The value of $S$ is not asserted.** The classical evaluation is a logarithm and
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-64-is not available at this point in the reading order; what is proved here is that
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-65-$S$ exists and where it lies. See
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md:66:[[rem-sums-proved-to-exist-but-not-evaluated]].
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-67-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-68-This is the series that gives the whole page its content: it is the standard
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-69-witness for [[fs-convergent-implies-absolutely-convergent]] and, through
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-70-[[thm-riemann-series-theorem]], the source of every rearrangement example below.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-71-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-72-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-73-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-74-**Given:** The alternating sequence $(\varepsilon_j)$ with index maps $e$ and $o$, the sequence $b_j = 1/\iota(j+1)$, and the partial sums $t_n = \sum_{j<n} \varepsilon_j b_j$ ([[def-series]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-75-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-76-[L1] The alternating sequence: $\varepsilon_0 = 1$, $\varepsilon_{j+1} = -\varepsilon_j$, $|\varepsilon_j| = 1$; $e_0 = 0$, $e_{j+1} = e_j + 2$, $o_0 = 1$, $o_{j+1} = o_j + 2$; $\varepsilon_{e_j} = 1$ and $\varepsilon_{o_j} = -1$ ([[lem-alternating-sequence]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-77-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-78-[L2] The canonical naturals are positive for $n \ge 1$ and strictly increasing; reciprocation reverses the order on the positives; and for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $n \ge 1$ with $1/\iota(n) < \varepsilon$ ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]], [[lem-of-inverse-positive]], [[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]]).
--
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1618-4.1 The $p$-series at $p = 1$ diverges, since $1 > 1$ is false; so $\sum |a_j|$ diverges. [step 3.2, L6]
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1619-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1620-5.1 Thus $\sum a_j$ converges while $\sum |a_j|$ does not, so $\sum a_j$ converges conditionally and not absolutely, and the claim [A1] fails for this series. [step 3.1, step 4.1, A1, L9]
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1621-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1622-6.1 The claim is therefore false. What survives of it is only the converse implication, that an absolutely convergent series converges. [step 5.1, A1, L10] ∎
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1623-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1624-## Remarks
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1625-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1626-- **The witness is not exotic.** It is the first series a reader meets whose convergence depends on cancellation, and the failure is as large as it can be: the series of absolute values does not merely converge to a different number, it diverges to $+\infty$.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1627-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1628-- **Everything on this page turns on this example.** Because the class of conditionally convergent series is nonempty, [[thm-riemann-series-theorem]] has content, and [[cor-unconditional-iff-absolute-in-r]] separates two genuinely different properties rather than restating one. The same series, with its rearrangements, is developed on the companion page.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md-1629-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-alternating-harmonic-series.md:1630:- **The value of the sum is not asserted here.** The series is proved convergent and nothing more; the classical evaluation needs the logarithm, which is later in the reading order ([[rem-sums-proved-to-exist-but-not-evaluated]]).
