research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-298-3. **The $(1-p_k)$ form.** Let $0 \le p_k < 1$ for every $k$. Then
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-299-   $\prod (1 - p_k)$ converges if and only if $\sum p_k$ converges; and if
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-300-   $\sum p_k$ diverges then $Q_n \to 0$, so that no tail of the product has
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-301-   partial products with a nonzero limit.
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-302-4. **Absolute convergence.** Let $(p_k)$ be an arbitrary sequence of reals with
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-303-   $\sum |p_k|$ convergent. Then $\prod (1 + p_k)$ converges.
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-304-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-305-**No logarithm occurs anywhere.** The exponential and the logarithm, through which
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-306-these criteria are usually derived, are later in the reading order; every
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-307-inequality above is an induction on finite products. The refinement that decides
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-308-$\prod(1+p_k)$ for signed $p_k$ with $\sum p_k$ convergent, in terms of the
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-309-convergence of $\sum p_k^2$, does need the logarithm and is not stated here; see
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md:310:[[rem-sums-proved-to-exist-but-not-evaluated]].
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-311-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-312-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-313-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-314-**Given:** A sequence $(p_k)$ of reals, with $S_n = \sum_{k<n}p_k$, $\Pi_n = \prod_{k<n}(1+p_k)$ and $Q_n = \prod_{k<n}(1-p_k)$.
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-315-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-316-[L1] Finite sums and products: $\sum_{k<0}x_k = 0$, $\prod_{k<0}x_k = 1$, $\sum_{k<n+1}x_k = \sum_{k<n}x_k + x_n$, $\prod_{k<n+1}x_k = \bigl(\prod_{k<n}x_k\bigr)x_n$, splitting at an intermediate index, and $\prod_{k<n}(x_ky_k) = \bigl(\prod_{k<n}x_k\bigr)\bigl(\prod_{k<n}y_k\bigr)$; a finite product of nonnegative factors is nonnegative and of positive factors is positive ([[def-finite-sum]], [[lem-finite-sum-laws]]).
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-317-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-318-[L2] The principle of induction on $\mathbb{N}$ ([[thm-induction-principle]]).
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-319-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-320-[L3] For a series of nonnegative terms: convergence is equivalent to the range of the partial sums being bounded above, the sum is then the supremum and every partial sum is at most the sum, and if the range is unbounded the partial sums diverge to $+\infty$ ([[thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums]], [[def-divergence-to-infinity]]).
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-321-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-infinite-product-criterion.md-322-[L4] A series converges if and only if some tail series converges, and then the sum equals the initial partial sum plus the tail sum ([[lem-series-tail-invariance]]).
--
