research/audit/wave8-dispatch/certifier-cex-cauchy-product-of-convergent-series-diverges.log-8899-## Remarks
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/certifier-cex-cauchy-product-of-convergent-series-diverges.log-8900-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/certifier-cex-cauchy-product-of-convergent-series-diverges.log-8901-- **Why the product cannot cancel.** By [[lem-alternating-sequence]] the sign of $a_k b_{n-k}$ is $\varepsilon_k\varepsilon_{n-k} = \varepsilon_n$, the same for every $k \le n$: the antidiagonal of the product array is sign-constant. So all $n+1$ terms of $c_n$ add, and the AM-GM bound ([[thm-am-gm]], with square roots as in [[thm-of-square-roots]]) shows each is at least $2/\iota(n+2)$.
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/certifier-cex-cauchy-product-of-convergent-series-diverges.log-8902-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/certifier-cex-cauchy-product-of-convergent-series-diverges.log-8903-- **Compare the geometric case.** In [[ex-cauchy-product-of-geometric-series]] the antidiagonal also has $n+1$ equal terms, but they are $r^{n}$ with $|r| < 1$, so the count $n+1$ is beaten by the decay. Here the terms of the antidiagonal are at least $2/\iota(n+2)$ each, and there are $n+1$ of them, so the count wins.
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/certifier-cex-cauchy-product-of-convergent-series-diverges.log-8904-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/certifier-cex-cauchy-product-of-convergent-series-diverges.log-8905-- **This is the same series as in [[cex-abel-test-needs-monotonicity]].** Its terms are exactly small enough to be summable with alternating signs and not otherwise, which is what makes it the standard witness for both failures.
