research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-ex-alternating-harmonic-rearranged-to-three-halves.prompt.md-2426-4.1 Fix $j$. For every $m \ge j$ one has $t_{e_j} \le t_{e_m}$, and $(t_{e_m})_m$ converges to $L$, so $t_{e_j} \le L$; symmetrically $t_{o_j} \ge L$. This is the first half of claim 2. [step 3.1, step 3.2, L3]
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-ex-alternating-harmonic-rearranged-to-three-halves.prompt.md-2427-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-ex-alternating-harmonic-rearranged-to-three-halves.prompt.md-2428-5.1 Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$. If $n = e_j$ then $t_n = t_{e_j} \le L$ and $t_{n+1} = t_{e_j+1} = t_{o_j} \ge L$; if $n = o_j$ then $t_n = t_{o_j} \ge L$ and $t_{n+1} = t_{o_j+1} = t_{e_{j+1}} \le L$. Since every $n$ is of exactly one of these two forms, $L$ always lies between $t_n$ and $t_{n+1}$, which is the second half of claim 2. [step 1.3, step 4.1, L1]
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-ex-alternating-harmonic-rearranged-to-three-halves.prompt.md-2429-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry1-ex-alternating-harmonic-rearranged-to-three-halves.prompt.md-2430-6.1 Consequently $|L - t_n| \le |t_{n+1} - t_n| = |\varepsilon_n b_n| = |\varepsilon_n|\,b_n = b_n$ for every $n$, using $b_n \ge 0$ and $|\varepsilon_n| = 1$; this is claim 3. [step 5.1, step 1.4, step 1.1, L1, L8] ∎
