library/real-analysis/the-exponential-function.md:29:The power series defines the exponential function and yields its addition law, positivity, derivative, monotonicity, limits, and range. Product limits, the normalized differential equation, the multiplicative functional equation, and Picard iteration are then identified with the same function. Factorial tail estimates give explicit bounds for $e$ and establish its irrationality, completing the comparison of these constructions without logarithms or arbitrary real powers.
research/plan-spec.json:3104:          "strategy": "the page's ledger, in the style of [[rem-sup-conventions]] and [[rem-metric-axiom-conventions]]. Record, each with the item that fixes it: (1) $\\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$, so every index range on this page starts at $0$ and $\\lvert A\\rvert$ may be $0$; (2) $\\lvert A\\rvert$ is defined for FINITE $A$ only and is a natural number, not a cardinal -- [[def-cardinal]] is a later and different object and nothing here uses it (forward reference, orientation only); (3) the empty sum is $0$, the empty product is $1$, $0! = 1$ and $0^{0} = 1$ are ONE convention, fixed by the base clauses of [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]], [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]] and [[def-nat-power]] and agreeing with [[def-finite-sum]] and [[def-integer-power]]; (4) $\\binom{n}{k}$ is a count and integrality is a theorem, [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]]; (5) three notions of finite sum now exist in the library -- over an initial segment ([[def-finite-sum]]), over a finite index set ([[def-sum-over-a-finite-index-set]]), and in a monoid ([[def-monoid-finite-product]], later) -- and the bridges are stated where each is introduced. WHAT IS NOT HERE, phrased as reading order and never as a claim about the library as a whole: inclusion-exclusion, the pigeonhole principle in its strong form, double counting as a theorem about relations, and the number of surjections all belong to the next page in this track; the binomial theorem for a commutative ring waits on rings; asymptotics of $n!$ waits on the integral and the logarithm. Forward references, all orientation only.",
