research/plan-spec.json:21160:          "strategy": "verification, and the cross-check is the point. Apply [[thm-integral-test-for-series]] to $f(t) = (t+1)^{-p}$ for rational $p>0$ ([[def-rational-power]], positive base, so the power is defined; $p$ MUST BE RATIONAL because real exponents do not exist in this library at this point in the reading order -- [[rem-real-exponents-deferred]]). Compute $\\int_0^N f$ by [[cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function]], separating $p = 1$ (where the primitive is not a power and the integral is not available here, so state only the boundedness question and resolve it by comparison) from $p \\ne 1$. Then compare the verdict with the published [[thm-p-series-rational]] and confirm they agree. If the $p=1$ case cannot be settled without the logarithm, say so and settle it by [[thm-cauchy-condensation]] instead, citing the published result rather than inventing a logarithm.",
research/plan-spec.json:22169:        "absolute-convergence-and-rearrangement",
research/plan-spec.json:22814:        "absolute-convergence-and-rearrangement",
research/plan-spec.json:23314:          "strategy": "Define $\\exp x=\\sum_{n\\ge0}x^n/\\iota(n!)$ and $e=\\exp(1)$. Keep the natural factorial of the finite-counting page distinct from its positive real embedding. The definition is provisional until convergence for every real $x$ is proved in the next item; do not use logarithms or real powers.",
research/plan-spec.json:23411:          "strategy": "Strict increase gives injectivity. Given $y>0$, the two endpoint limits give $a<b$ with $\\exp(a)<y<\\exp(b)$, and the intermediate-value theorem gives a preimage. Positivity excludes every nonpositive value. Do not name the inverse logarithm on this page.",
research/plan-spec.json:23488:          "strategy": "Take $n>|x|$ so the base is positive and expand by the binomial theorem. For a fixed finite head, the scaled-binomial lemma gives convergence to the corresponding exponential terms. Bound the remaining binomial tail uniformly by the tail of $\\sum |x|^k/k!$, then choose the head by convergence of that series. This finite-head/infinite-tail proof works for negative $x$ and contains no logarithm.",
research/plan-spec.json:23582:          "strategy": "Record the five equivalent constructions proved here and the proof order: series before addition, addition before positivity, derivative before IVP uniqueness, and all of these before characterization. State that logarithms and arbitrary real powers are deferred. Distinguish the published rational-power operation from $\\exp(x\\log a)$, which is unavailable. Record that the compact bump is in the companion page but a general smooth partition of unity and a nowhere-analytic smooth function are outside this dependency-closed scope.",
research/plan-spec.json:23607:          "strategy": "Apply the product-limit theorem at $x=-2$, noting that the base is positive for $n>2$. Expand several finite cases only as a sanity check; the proof uses the general finite-head/tail theorem, not a nonexistent logarithm of a negative or zero base.",
