research/plan-spec.json-21835-          "title": "Steinitz's polygonal confinement theorem: finitely many vectors of norm at most $1$ summing to $0$ can be ordered so that every partial sum has norm at most $n$",
research/plan-spec.json-21836-          "strategy": "constructive, and the proof is fully available from published material; scaffold it in five named stages and check each. STATEMENT: let $n \\ge 1$, let $v : m \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ with $\\lVert v_i\\rVert_2 \\le 1$ for every $i<m$ and $\\sum_{i<m} v_i = 0$; then there is a bijection $\\pi : m \\to m$ with $\\lVert\\sum_{i<k} v_{\\pi(i)}\\rVert_2 \\le \\iota(n)$ for every $k \\le m$. (1) If $m \\le n$ every partial sum has norm at most $\\iota(k) \\le \\iota(n)$ by the triangle inequality; assume $m > n$. (2) For $n \\le k \\le m$ let $\\Lambda_k := \\{\\lambda : m \\to [0,1] \\mid \\sum_i \\lambda_i v_i = 0,\\ \\sum_i \\lambda_i = \\iota(k-n)\\}$ RELATIVE TO A SUBSET $A_k$ (see 3). $\\Lambda_m$ contains the constant $\\iota(m-n)/\\iota(m)$, since $\\sum v_i = 0$. (3) DESCENDING CONSTRUCTION: build $A_m \\supseteq A_{m-1} \\supseteq \\dots \\supseteq A_n$ with $\\lvert A_k\\rvert = k$ and $\\lambda^{k}$ supported in $A_k$, in $[0,1]^{A_k}$, with $\\sum_{i \\in A_k}\\lambda^k_i v_i = 0$ and $\\sum_{i\\in A_k}\\lambda^k_i = \\iota(k-n)$. Given $A_k,\\lambda^k$, the feasible set for $k-1$ is nonempty (scale $\\lambda^k$ by $\\iota(k-1-n)/\\iota(k-n)$; the case $k-n = 0$ does not arise since the construction stops at $k = n$). (4) THE KEY LEMMA, and it is pure linear algebra: among the feasible $\\lambda$ choose one with the FEWEST coordinates in the open interval $(0,1)$ -- legitimate because a nonempty set of naturals has a least element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]). If that number were at least $n+2$, the vectors $(v_i,1) \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ indexed by the fractional coordinates would be more than $\\dim \\mathbb{R}^{n+1} = n+1$ in number, hence linearly dependent ([[cor-independent-set-is-no-larger-than-a-finite-spanning-set]], [[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]], [[def-dimension]]); a nontrivial dependence $\\mu$ gives $\\sum\\mu_i v_i = 0$ and $\\sum\\mu_i = 0$, and moving $\\lambda + t\\mu$ to the nearest $t$ at which some fractional coordinate reaches $0$ or $1$ (a minimum over a nonempty finite set of reals, [[lem-finite-set-has-max]]) keeps feasibility and strictly reduces the count -- contradiction. So at most $n+1$ coordinates are fractional. (5) THE SUPPORT BOUND, the step that makes the nesting work and the one an inattentive proof omits: $\\#\\{i : \\lambda_i = 1\\} \\le \\sum_i\\lambda_i = k-1-n$, so $\\#\\mathrm{supp}(\\lambda) \\le (k-1-n)+(n+1) = k$; and equality would force the fractional coordinates to sum to $0$ while each is strictly positive, which is impossible, so $\\#\\mathrm{supp}(\\lambda) \\le k-1$ and an $A_{k-1} \\subseteq A_k$ of size $k-1$ containing the support exists. (6) THE ORDERING: enumerate $A_n$ first in any order, then the single element of $A_{k}\\setminus A_{k-1}$ for $k = n+1,\\dots,m$ in increasing $k$. For $k \\le n$ the partial sum has norm at most $\\iota(k)\\le\\iota(n)$; for $k \\ge n$ the partial sum is $\\sum_{i\\in A_k} v_i = \\sum_{i\\in A_k}(1-\\lambda^k_i)v_i$, whose norm is at most $\\sum_{i\\in A_k}(1-\\lambda^k_i) = \\iota(k) - \\iota(k-n) = \\iota(n)$. Every sum here is a sum over a finite index set; if the counting page's index-set sum is not among this page's declared prerequisites, index by a chosen enumeration and use [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] instead. LANDMARK.",
