research/plan-spec.json:21864:          "id": "thm-rearrangement-sums-lie-in-an-affine-subspace",
research/plan-spec.json-21865-          "kind": "theorem",
research/plan-spec.json-21866-          "title": "The set of rearrangement sums of a convergent series in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a nonempty subset of the affine subspace $s + \\Gamma^{\\perp}$",
research/plan-spec.json-21867-          "strategy": "direct, and it is the containment half of the Levy-Steinitz theorem; SCOPE THE CLAIM EXACTLY AND DO NOT OVERSTATE THE TITLE, since a title claiming more than the proof gives is a fatal defect. Let $\\sum x_k$ converge to $s$. Nonemptiness: $s \\in \\mathcal{S}(x)$, via the identity bijection. Containment: let $t \\in \\mathcal{S}(x)$, so $\\sum x_{\\sigma(k)} = t$ for some bijection $\\sigma$, and let $a \\in \\Gamma$. The real series $\\sum\\langle a,x_k\\rangle$ converges absolutely by definition of $\\Gamma$, so by the published [[thm-dirichlet-rearrangement]] every rearrangement of it has the same sum; and $\\langle a,\\cdot\\rangle$ is continuous ([[thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity]], or directly by Cauchy-Schwarz) and additive on finite sums ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 1), so $\\langle a,t\\rangle = \\sum_k\\langle a,x_{\\sigma(k)}\\rangle = \\sum_k\\langle a,x_k\\rangle = \\langle a,s\\rangle$. Hence $\\langle a, t-s\\rangle = 0$ for every $a \\in \\Gamma$, that is $t - s \\in \\Gamma^{\\perp}$. State the two extreme cases as clauses, because they are exactly the published one-dimensional dichotomy seen from here: if the series converges absolutely then $\\Gamma = \\mathbb{R}^n$, $\\Gamma^{\\perp} = \\{0\\}$ and $\\mathcal{S}(x) = \\{s\\}$ ([[thm-absolute-convergence-in-rn]]); at $n = 1$ a conditionally convergent series has $\\Gamma = \\{0\\}$ and $\\Gamma^{\\perp} = \\mathbb{R}$, and the published [[thm-riemann-series-theorem]] says the containment is then an EQUALITY. WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED, and must be said in Remarks in exactly these terms: this theorem gives containment only; whether $\\mathcal{S}(x)$ is all of $s+\\Gamma^{\\perp}$ for $n \\ge 2$ is not settled here, and no assertion about it, in either direction, is made anywhere on this page. Declare [[rem-rearrangement-in-higher-dimensions]] nowhere in `deps` -- it is a published remark on an earlier page and the edge runs the other way, as a forward reference to be added there. LANDMARK.",
