research/plan-spec.json:21894:          "strategy": "the page ledger, and on this page it carries more weight than usual because three separate scope decisions were taken. Record: (1) THE $n \\ge 1$ HYPOTHESIS, where it comes from ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]] defines $d_\\infty$ only there) and exactly which items inherit it -- list them; note that [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and the vector space structure are fine at $n = 0$, so the boundary is between the algebra and the metric, not between statements a reader would guess. (2) THE EXPONENT IN $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_p$ IS RATIONAL, because real exponents do not exist at this point in the reading order ([[rem-real-exponents-deferred]]); every $p$-norm statement on this page carries it. (3) $\\mathbb{R}^n$ IS A FUNCTION SPACE, the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]], [[def-function-space]]), so $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is not literally $\\mathbb{R}$ and every comparison with the one-dimensional theory goes through the stated identification. (4) WHAT IS TAKEN UP ELSEWHERE, phrased as reading order and never as a claim about the library as a whole: the operator norm and the fact that linear maps are Lipschitz belong with linear maps, which are introduced on a page earlier in plan order that is not yet built; the general theory of inner product spaces and orthogonality likewise; uniform convergence, the total derivative and the integral in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ all come later in this track. (5) THE OPEN HALF OF THE REARRANGEMENT QUESTION: [[thm-rearrangement-sums-lie-in-an-affine-subspace]] proves containment and the page asserts nothing about the reverse inclusion for $n \\ge 2$; the published [[rem-rearrangement-in-higher-dimensions]] on the series page raised the question and this page answers the part it can. State that plainly; do NOT state the classical answer, in any form, and do not create a recorded-not-proved item for it.",
