research/plan-spec.json:22019:          "strategy": "verification, and it makes an abstract existence proof concrete. Take six vectors of norm at most $1$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ summing to $0$ -- for instance $\\pm e_0, \\pm e_1$ together with $\\pm(1,1)/\\sqrt{2}$ -- exhibit an ordering whose partial sums all have norm at most $\\iota(2)$, and exhibit a BAD ordering whose partial sums exceed $1$, so that the theorem is seen to say something. Then trace one step of the descending construction of [[thm-steinitz-polygonal-confinement]] on this list: write down $A_6$, one $\\lambda^{6}$, and the resulting $A_5$, checking the support bound $\\#\\mathrm{supp} \\le k-1$ that the proof turns on. An example that only asserts the bound would be worth much less than one that exhibits the construction.",
