research/plan-spec.json:22035:          "strategy": "computation, and it is the example that makes [[def-the-space-of-summing-directions]] concrete. Take $x_k := \\bigl((-1)^{k}/\\iota(k+1),\\ 0\\bigr)$: the series converges by the alternating series test ([[thm-alternating-series-test]]) in the first coordinate and trivially in the second. For $a = (a_0,a_1)$, $\\langle a,x_k\\rangle = a_0(-1)^{k}/\\iota(k+1)$, so $\\sum\\lvert\\langle a,x_k\\rangle\\rvert$ converges iff $a_0 = 0$ (harmonic divergence, [[thm-p-series-rational]] at $p=1$ or [[thm-cauchy-condensation]]). Hence $\\Gamma = \\{(0,a_1)\\}$, a line, and $\\Gamma^{\\perp} = \\{(y_0,0)\\}$, a line. So [[thm-rearrangement-sums-lie-in-an-affine-subspace]] confines every rearrangement sum to the horizontal line through $s$ -- and, since the second coordinate is identically $0$, that confinement is visibly sharp in the trivial direction. Note what this example does NOT decide: whether every point of that line is attained. Say so; do not assert it.",
