research/plan-spec.json-22054-          "title": "FALSE: if a convergent series in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ does not converge absolutely, then every point of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the sum of some rearrangement of it",
research/plan-spec.json:22055:          "strategy": "refutation, and it is the item that stops a reader generalising [[thm-riemann-series-theorem]] by analogy -- the exact expectation the published [[rem-rearrangement-in-higher-dimensions]] warns against. Use the series of [[ex-gamma-and-its-complement-computed-for-a-plane-series]]: it converges, it does not converge absolutely (its norms are $1/\\iota(k+1)$, whose series diverges), and every rearrangement has second coordinate $0$, so no point off the horizontal line through $s$ is a rearrangement sum. That refutes the statement without needing any part of the classical higher-dimensional theorem, which is the point: the refutation is elementary and complete. Note that [[thm-rearrangement-sums-lie-in-an-affine-subspace]] explains it structurally, $\\Gamma^{\\perp}$ being a proper subspace, and that the one-dimensional case is genuinely different because there $\\Gamma = \\{0\\}$ forces $\\Gamma^{\\perp} = \\mathbb{R}$.",
